          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What blood pressure target does guidance describe for most adults?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not clearly give a single blood pressure target for most adults, so I can't name one from these sources.\n\n**What the sources do say:**\n\n- **Classification (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\"):** Office readings are classed as normal (below 120/80), elevated (120 to 129 systolic and below 80 diastolic), stage 1 (130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic), or stage 2 (140 or more systolic, or 90 or more diastolic). The higher category applies when the two numbers fall in different ones. This document says it covers \"the treatment target most bodies set,\" but the text I received cut off before stating that target.\n- **People with coronary artery disease (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention\"):** The target is below 130/80 mm Hg for most people. This applies to people who already have that diagnosis, so it is not a general target for all adults.\n\n**What I could not confirm:** I did not retrieve the general treatment target for adults without heart disease, and I did not retrieve the section on where authorities disagree about it. The hypertension document does have a disagreement section, but its content was not in the passages returned. I won't fill that gap from memory. A clinician, or the full guideline text, can give the current general target and explain any differences between authorities.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
